## Triage Summary

Merged ticket **#4471** contains **427 reports** across **6 distinct problems**. The original desk classification treated this as one issue, but secondary analysis reveals multiple independent bugs affecting date and time handling in `sched-prod`.

## Problems Identified

### 1. Wrong Day of Week on Invite — 111 reports

**Severity**: High  
**Status**: Open ([GitHub #1](https://github.com/OldMetalmind/s01e03-ticket-4471/issues/1))

Calendar invites display incorrect day-of-week names, causing customers to arrive on the wrong day even when the underlying date is correct.

**Example**: Booking for Monday 11 May gets invite saying "Tuesday 12 May". Customer arrives Monday; appointment is missed.

**Affected locales**: en-GB (27), en-US (12), pl-PL (10), nl-NL (9), da-DK (9), and others.

**Root cause**: Day-of-week calculation bug in invite generation or date serialization.

---

### 2. Month and Day Swapped in Locale Format — 53 reports

**Severity**: Medium  
**Status**: Open ([GitHub #2](https://github.com/OldMetalmind/s01e03-ticket-4471/issues/2))

Dates show with month and day reversed for specific locales, suggesting inconsistent DD/MM vs MM/DD parsing.

**Example**: "Every booking in June is one day off. Only June."

**Affected locales**: en-GB (15), it-IT (7), de-DE (4), pl-PL (4), fr-FR (4), pt-PT (4), en-US (4).

**Root cause**: Locale-aware date format parsing error. Possibly mixing CLDR locale data with hardcoded format strings.

---

### 3. Booking Date Mismatch Between UI and Invite — 53 reports

**Severity**: High  
**Status**: Open ([GitHub #3](https://github.com/OldMetalmind/s01e03-ticket-4471/issues/3))

The date shown in the booking confirmation differs completely from the date on the invite. Not just a display issue—the underlying data is inconsistent between systems.

**Example**: "Customer booked 3 October and got an invite for 10 March."

**Root cause**: Data inconsistency between booking system and invite generation:
- Different timezone handling between systems
- Race condition during booking/invite creation  
- Incorrect date transformation in one system

---

### 4. Appointment Auto-Moved Without User Action — 34 reports

**Severity**: Critical  
**Status**: Open ([GitHub #4](https://github.com/OldMetalmind/s01e03-ticket-4471/issues/4))

Appointments automatically change date/time by several months without user action. This is a **data integrity issue** that destroys customer trust.

**Example**: Appointment moves from 7 April to 4 July (3 months forward).

**Root cause**: Unknown. Suspects:
- Background job with incorrect date recalculation logic
- Database trigger with wrong math
- Scheduled task that runs unexpectedly

**Investigation**: Check cron jobs, background workers, and data migration logs from April onwards.

---

### 5. Reminder Sent on Wrong Date — 37 reports

**Severity**: Medium  
**Status**: Open ([GitHub #5](https://github.com/OldMetalmind/s01e03-ticket-4471/issues/5))

Appointment reminders are sent weeks or months before/after the actual appointment.

**Example**: "Customer booked 6 November and got an invite for 11 June."

**Root cause**: Reminder scheduling uses wrong date:
- Timezone mismatch
- Date format parsing error
- Off-by-one or off-by-month calculation

---

### 6. Generic Date/Time Issues — 139 reports

**Severity**: Medium  
**Status**: Open ([GitHub #6](https://github.com/OldMetalmind/s01e03-ticket-4471/issues/6))

139 reports (32% of merged ticket) don't fit cleanly into categories above. Likely contain:
- Additional timezone issues
- Locale-specific edge cases
- Recurring patterns not yet identified

**Next step**: Secondary triage required on sample of 20-30 reports to find patterns and potentially split into sub-issues.

---

## Coverage

- **Categorized**: 288 reports (67%)
- **Unclassified**: 139 reports (33%)
- **Total**: 427 reports

